      "dom_id": "question-162705",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14191838/assets/897d0472039c499aee543a320f74b13bf1887f8b170ce1167a6c808d933ec676.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,2),$ so the global maximum point is $(1,2,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14191838/assets/690d407982bfa9cde8fdce5b187006af1c24f9080a0cfea77e7551ca1d5cd940.png)",
